[{:seon.task/name "a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"
  :seon.task/plan "Census #2 (notes/AUDIT-2026-10-21-laws.md), audits A, C #2, #16. A step that throws loses the request it took: Flow's step catch (jolt stdlib clojure.core.async.flow.impl, the proc loop) sends {::flow/pid ::flow/msg ::flow/ex ...} to the error channel, keeps the old state and drops the outputs, and root's error reader (the seon-flow-errors thread in `seon.root/start!`) forwards only {:op :error :pid :message :data} to the api, so `seon.root/back-op`'s :error branch records the defect but answers nobody. Callers therefore grew deadlines with fake fallbacks: `seon.root/state`, `seon.root/stats`, `seon.root/errors` and `seon.root/agent-entry` answer {} after (deref ... 5000 {}), so `seon.entrance/agents-answer` reports no live agents and `seon.root/merge!` refuses \"no agent X\" while root is merely busy (\"Unknown is not {}\", AGENTS.md section 8); `seon.web/ask!` answers nil after 10 s (a 503); `seon.root/fs-call!` answers :timeout after 30 s; `seon.render.proc/ASK-BOUND-MS` re-asks after 2 s. Correct: (1) the error reader forwards the message the step took as :msg; (2) `seon.root/back-op`'s :error branch answers that message's caller with the defect's error map: its :p when it came in at the entrance, else the api request pending under its :dest's :rid (`seon.root/deliver-rid`); (3) a defect in the api's own catch-up loop (`seon.root/api-proc` takes several queued messages in one step, and Flow names only the first) is answered for every message that step took: the api answers each message it takes before taking the next, or names them all on the error; (4) callers park on the reply: every timed deref above becomes a plain deref, and the answer passes through as itself, so `seon.root/state` answers the error map undecorated (no :main, :writer, :store merged around it) and `seon.root/errors` declares the error variant in its contract ([:or :seon.error/fold :seon.error/base], written by the design step); its caller `my.root/problems` branches on it. Deletes the four 5000 ms derefs and their {} fallbacks, the 10 s and 30 s fallbacks, and `seon.render.proc/ASK-BOUND-MS` with its re-ask. The writer's own catch in `seon.writer/writer-one` (an interop edge, answering the request it was on) is not this task. A timed deref left behind is the clock census's finding (seon.fences/the-dragon-gate-sees-fences); no proof here can see a 5 s timeout without a clock. Estimate: no new work; one answer per lost request. Lane ANSWERS, 2026-10-22 (on jolt/shrink-ask, which deleted `seon.render.proc/ASK-BOUND-MS` and its re-ask): done (1)-(3) and (4) in root: the error reader forwards `::flow/msg` as :msg; the :error branch of `seon.root/back-op` answers it through `seon.root/answer-taken` (its :dest through back-op itself, so a lost :spawn, :merged or :errors-flushed answer is answered too; its :p or :route; its :rid; an answer a proc took is answered again on [pid :answers], never one that was already an error); the api keeps what each step took in `::taken`, a volatile in its state that Flow's revert keeps, moves it to `::lost` at the next step when the step threw, and answers all of it at its defect; the render proc takes an error answer to its view request without rendering it. `seon.root/state`, `stats`, `errors` and `agent-entry` deref plainly; `state` answers a defect undecorated. Left: `seon.web/ask!`'s 10 s fallback (web.clj, not this lane's file); `my.root/problems` does not yet branch on `seon.root/errors` answering a defect (my/root.clj); the writer's and the stand-ins' own batches (a writer step that throws past its own catch names only its first request; an agent stand-in's frames name their rid as :id or :k, which `answer-taken` does not read); Flow's error channel is a sliding(100) buffer, so a defect dropped there is never answered."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-throwing-step-answers-every-pending-request
                      seon.procs-test/root-state-never-answers-an-empty-system}}
 {:seon.task/name "no-head-of-line-blocking"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit D F1 (reproduced): one busy agent plus one chatty agent stalled root's whole api ~25 s while health said :running: the api writes [agent :in] with Flow's blocking send (agent_proc.clj:19, 64 slots + 1 MB socket). The api never blocks on one agent (the stand-in holds overflow in its state and admits what fits); health reports progress (the oldest unanswered request), not liveness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow"
  :seon.task/plan "Audits A #2-3,#6-8, D F3-F5,F8: shutdown is four nested time bounds and two SIGKILL ladders with nothing recorded (root.clj:806-848, agent_proc.clj:42-56); child death is seen only by a 100 ms idle poll in the fork server (fork_server.clj:92-93), so deaths during a commit stream and after a failover are lost and a promoted server leaves a zombie. Child death is an event (kqueue EVFILT_PROC / SIGCHLD self-pipe in the fork server's poll); a death is recorded at socket EOF, its exit status added when known; spawn and clone are refused while closing; root parks on the drain; an agent that does not drain is a recorded problem; the conn and LMDB env belong to the writer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-death-is-recorded-at-once-and-its-status-added
                      seon.procs-test/shutdown-drains-without-a-kill}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-clock"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit A #13-16: the stall timer is a go block started inside the api's step (root.clj:449-457), the rate bucket a callback take! that forks an OS thread per pending tick in Jolt (fx.clj:93-104), the dashboard beat a raw thread (render/proc.clj:49-64). One declared :clock proc emits ticks on bounded channels; the stall and rate dials are named dragons."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-clock-is-the-only-time-source}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-threads-are-a-known-number"
  :seon.task/plan "bin/check lifecycle (10 start!/stop! cycles in one process) went red once on main (2026-10-21, 0b9df34, load ~8 with six lanes building): root's OS threads 25,25,25,26,26,29,29,29,29,29 against its bound of +2. Three reruns passed but drift the same way: 26->28, 25->27, flat 31. Steps then a plateau: a pool growing to a size that varies by run, not a leak per cycle. A thread count should be known: name each thread root starts (flow procs by workload, the fork-server reader, the flusher, the web/MCP listeners, any executor pool) and the number each owner owns, so the check asserts the exact count after each cycle instead of a growth bound. Find which pool grows and why its size depends on the run (lazy growth under contention?); fix it to a declared size, or record it as a declared, bounded pool. FOUND (lane THREADS): Jolt's threads stayed flat (42 across 9 cycles in one root) while the OS count grew by Chez's parallel-GC sweepers (sample: start_sweeper), 0 -> 5 -> 15: setup_sweepers (chez c/gc.c:2988) starts one per thread a collection finds waiting and keeps it, so the count follows load. Chez has no knob (parallel collection is automatic, release notes 10.0.0); our Chez (jolt/gc-sweepers) adds (cs)collect_sweepers n, and root fixes cfg/GC-SWEEPERS right after forking the fork server. Also: Jolt's runtime threads are named (jolt-carrier-N, jolt-timer, jolt-shutdown, ...); put!/take! that cannot complete are channel waiters run on fibers, not a thread each (Jolt c508734d); render is :mixed, not futurized onto the cached :compute pool; seon.root/declared-threads names every thread and lifecycle, scale and threads-declared assert the exact roster (seon.check/thread-diff)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by lane LOOP (2026-10-21, worktree next-loop at 2a079e5): `bin/root start` on the worktree's kept store runs/store (created 14:56 by an intermediate build of jolt/loop-program, one with the uncontracted seed) booted and listened. `(my.root/load-tasks! sys dir)` as root answered (a refusal for another file). Then `{:op :agents}` answered `{:error \"seon.task/entity-value invalid-output: its value does not satisfy its contract\"}` with the problem `{:path [0 0 0] :expected [:= :task] :value \":root\"}` (the :or's first branch; the :root branch's problem was cut from the printed answer), and from then on no `{:op :eval :agent \"root\"}` was answered (not even `(+ 1 2)`, nc waited 60 s), RSS 1.7 GB and 0% CPU; `bin/root stop` still stopped it. A second boot on the same store hung on its first root eval. A root on a fresh store (SEON_RUNS=runs/loop/rootruns) answered every request. Cause not traced: `seon.task/entity-value` on a db written by the test harness with `seon.task/boot-tx` and `seon.config/facts` answers `[:root {...}]` (probe at the REPL), so the store's :root entity (written by the older build) is the suspect, and the hang is root-eval's deref of `seon.root/transact-main!` after the reconciler's step threw on it (a proc that throws must still answer: `seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took`). Keep runs/store in next-loop for the reproduction. Correct: a root resumed on any store this repository's builds wrote answers `:agents` and evals, and an entity it cannot read is an error fact naming it, never a silent stall. Needed before `seon.source/the-next-generation-runs-from-the-merges-commit`, which resumes the store a previous generation wrote. Lane NEXTGEN, 2026-10-21: fixed by two landings, `seon.db/a-kept-store-gains-new-attributes-at-open` (`seon.db/install-missing!`; a refused boot fact ends the boot, `seon.root/start!`) and `seon.error/a-repeated-defect-writes-nothing`. The shape was read from a kept store of the same day's earlier builds (next/runs/store-full-20261021, opened read-only from an APFS clone): its schema lacks 108 of the 232 attributes the current schema declares (the config's, the model manifest's, the source's), and main run-20261021T144308-dd0ab3bc's root entity holds `:seon.root/quiescent` and no `:seon.root/boot` (its boot fact was refused and ignored), so `seon.task/root` answers nil and `seon.task/entity-value` on it is LOOP's invalid-output, reproduced at the REPL on that store. The proof builds that store (schema less the later attributes, the seed, that root entity on main :old-run), resumes it with the built root (`--set` store-path and main) and asks a root eval, `{:op :agents}` and the platform problem count: green, 3 / {:agents {} :tasks {}} / 0, boot to answers ~1 s. With `seon.db/install-missing!` disabled it is red: the boot fact is refused and the boot ends. With the refusal ignored as well (both landings' boot halves out) it is LOOP's symptom without the hang: the eval answers 3, `{:op :agents}` answers `Bad entity attribute :seon.config.agent/max-running ... not defined in current schema`, 2 platform problems; the hang itself (a defect writing a commit per occurrence) is what `seon.error/a-repeated-defect-writes-nothing` removed, and was not reproduced again. The proof stays as the regression."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote}}

 {:seon.task/name "one-main-resumed"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan notes/PLAN-branches.md (owner, 2026-10-22). `seon.root/start!` names its main `run-<time>-<id>` unless `:seon.config.root/main` is set, and `bin/root` never sets it, so the standing store holds 68 mains and every restart starts over (tasks unloaded, refinement budgets reset). Correct: `:seon.config.root/main` defaults to `main` in `resources/seon/config/default.edn` (documented), every boot resumes it (a new store creates it from the seed), and `:seon.config.root/run-name` with the minting in `seon.root/start!` is deleted. A run's record is main's commit history. Proof: a root started twice on one store resumes the same main, its tasks and attempts still there. Lane ONEMAIN, 2026-10-22: `seon.root/start!` resumes `(cfg/value :seon.config.root/main)` (default.edn `#or [#env SEON_MAIN \"main\"]`); `:seon.config.root/run-name`, its schema entries and the stamp minting are deleted; `bin/check`'s SEON_RUN became SEON_MAIN=check-<step>. A resumed main's reconciler reaps the earlier boot's attempt and starts the next one, so the proof asserts the first boot's attempt is still there, not the attempt count."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/a-second-boot-resumes-the-same-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-end-of-a-root-is-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "From jolt/door (677b529, lane DOOR, 2026-10-21), re-targeted to main after wave 2 (e117b9b). A root's end leaves nothing on main: `seon.root/stop!` writes no fact, and a boot cannot tell a clean stop from a SIGKILL or a crash, so a kept run does not say how it ended. Correct: `seon.root/stop!` first writes `:seon.root/stopped {:boot :cause :at-ms}` on main, durable (`:flush? true`; cause `:stop!` unless the caller names it), and a refused stop fact still stops root (its answer carries it); `seon.check/root-serve` names its cause: `:stdin-eof` (bin/root stop closes the holder's pipe) or `:signal` (SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP through Jolt's shutdown hooks, which wait for the stop); `seon.root/start!` writes `:seon.root/previous-end` with its boot fact: the previous boot's stop fact, or `:unclean` when it left none. Attributes in seon.facts.edn as plain data (`:seon.root/end`); a kept store gains them at open (`seon.db/install-missing!`). bin/check durable-read asserts the SIGKILLed phase-1 root ended `:unclean`. Not the branch's door stop (`{:op :stop}`, `:door-stop`): main's bin/root stops through stdin. Write set: `seon.root/stop!`, `seon.root/start!`, `seon.root/previous-end` (new), `seon.check/root-serve`, `seon.check/durable-read`. Wiki: docs/parity.md#operating-the-system (bin/root stop row) and DRAGONS (a SIGKILLed root is found :unclean)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/every-end-of-a-root-is-a-fact}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refusal-is-the-one-error-map"
  :seon.task/plan "Lane DUP-ROOT, audit rows B1 and B2 (notes/duplicates-audit-2026-10-22.md), verified on main c3265a8. (B1) Root answers a request no live agent can take with fabricated protocol members: `seon.root/no-agent` answers an :eval `[{:id -1 :tag :err :err e :ms 0.0 :alloc-bytes 0}]`, :tests `(assoc e :pass 0 :fail 0 :error 1 :reports ...)`, a merge `:merged? false`, anything else `:ok false`; `seon.root/request` adds `{:ok false :applied [] :conflicts []}` and `{:merged? false}`; `seon.writer/refused` merges `{:ok false}` into every refusal and the merge refusals add `:merged? false`; a red gated merge (`seon.agent-proc`, the :merge-tests answer) is `{:merged? false :tests :reports}`, not an error at all. The readers then patch: the reconciler detects a death by `(= -1 (:id (first r)))` (:worked) and `seon.reconcile/error-text` strips `:pass :fail :error :reports :merged? :ok :tx :applied :conflicts` back out (it also strips a real `:tx`); `seon.root/failed` needs `(false? (:merged? r))` beside `contract/error?`. (B2) A second error shape `{:error \"string\"}`: the agent view's `:error` and the `{:op :failed :error msg}` sent to the agent (`seon.root` :fork-failed, :server-down, :clone-failed), `seon.agent-proc` status `{:error ...}` and `:clone-failed :error string`, `seon.agent` `:clone-failed` from lib/fork!'s refused branch and the `:branched` answer's `:error`; root re-wraps the string as `contract/error :seon.agent/failed` when the agent's status reports it, and `seon.render.ui/agent-card` reads the string. The cause: no rule that a refusal is only the error map. Correct, at each producer: a refusal is `seon.contract/error` alone, carrying only members that describe the refusal (`:agent`, `:behind`, `:uncontracted`, a red run's `:tests`/`:reports` under a typed `:seon.merge/red`); no-agent, writer's `{:ok false}` and request's fake members are deleted; every asker branches on `contract/error?` (the reconciler's :worked arm calls `dead` with the error, its other arms already do; `seon.entrance` :eval and :context answer the error; `seon.root/failed` reads `contract/error?` alone; `seon.render.ui` bad-event? reads `contract/error?` of the result; bin/check's smoke, durable and robust steps read the error rather than `:pass`/`(:err (last r))`); error-text is deleted and the attempt stores the error map as it is (`:seon.attempt/error` is a `:map`). The B2 producers make `contract/error` (the agent view's `:error` holds the error map, so root's re-wrap goes), readers read `:seon.error/message` only. Not changed: `seon.reconcile` `(:error end)` is the end's `:seon.end/error` field (an error map) under pull-end's short name, not the second shape (the audit and dup-views' hand-over list it as a dual reader: wrong); `:seon.root/source`'s `:error` and `:seon.root/generation`'s `:error` are stored fact fields, left. What v1 did: one error map (`seon.error`). Deletes about 25 lines. Proofs written red first: procs_test's :tx refusal and no-live-agent :eval assert the bare error map; problems_test's died-in-an-eval reads the error; the reconciler records a death's cause when the turn loop's agent died (today it loses the cause and records the follow-up's \"no live agent\")."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-refusal-is-the-one-error-map seon.reconcile-test/an-agent-dead-in-its-turn-loop-records-its-cause seon.problems-test/an-agent-death-is-recorded-with-its-cause seon.problems-test/root-records-what-went-wrong-in-an-agent seon.agent-test/an-agent-that-throws-ends-with-its-cause}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-keeps-what-is-used"
  :seon.task/plan "Lane DUP-ROOT, audit rows C1, C2, C3, C8, A1, A3, A19, A20, B3 and C10, verified on main c3265a8. (C1) `seon.root/agent-ping` is gone but its route stays with no producer: the `:ping` request kind (`seon.root` api case and msg), `seon.agent-proc`'s `:pings` queue and `:pong` arm, the pong in `seon.agent/await-op!`; delete them (step/run-loop's control ping stays). (C3) the ext-op `:message` arm has no producer (agent messages arrive on the back port's arm, which stays): delete. (C2, audit wrong: `seon.root/stats` is called by bin/check) and (C8) the check-only root API: `stats` is a field of `seon.root/state`, `pause-agent!` and `resume-agent!` are Flow's `pause-proc`/`resume-proc`; check reads those directly and the wrappers go (`agent-health` and `health` were already gone). `fs-call!`, `agent-stats!`, `branch-diff` need root's entrance, and lib's `summ`, `gc-stats`, `sysmem` are only check's: moving them into check saves no src line until check leaves src (`seon.main/drivers-leave-src`), so they stay until then. An ext request of a kind root does not know is answered refused (it was left unanswered). (A1) the four hand readers `(get-in contract/forms [k 1 :seon.status/...])` (`seon.root/gone-statuses`, `stopped-statuses`, `seon.reconcile/finished`, `judged`) read `seon.contract/enum-values`, and seon.render.ui's copies use root's and reconcile's. (A3) root's entity is read by pull of its lookup ref everywhere except `seon.root/previous-end`, a Datalog query: a pull (dup-helpers found the one helper unneeded). (A19) the fork server's stop is written twice (stop transition and :shutdown): one private `release!`. (A20) `(:seon.error/type x)` as a predicate at nine sites (turn, template, my.root, fx, model, agent) reads `contract/error?`. (B3) fake defaults: `seon.reconcile/pull-end` answers `{}` for no end (nil), root's `{:boot \"\"}`, `(:seon.attempt/outcome a :error)`, `(::errors st {})`, the health `:unknown`s (they move with C8), and the clone refusal that says \"no live agent\" when the cause is too many agents (two refusals, each its reason). (C10) keys decoded with `clojure.core/read-string` (program, harvest) and `clojure.edn/read-string` beside `seon.edn/decode` (program, source) decode through seon.edn. Each is a delete or move; existing tests and bin/check stay green. Deletes about 45 lines in place, about 40 move to check."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-refusal-is-the-one-error-map seon.reconcile-test/an-attempt-without-an-end-reads-nil}}
 {:seon.task/name "start-takes-a-config-layer"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed by lane DUP-ROOT (audit row B6, root's half), 2026-10-22, by root's ruling: a design change, not built in the lane. `seon.root/start!` takes `:max-running`, `:provider` and `:stall-ms` as options beside the config, and `seon.config/with-provider` folds a provider into the config written on main's root entity; bin/check (`:max-running 2 :stall-ms 1000`) and seon.e2e pass them. The written config is what the reconciler reads (main's root fact), so nothing bypasses main; what is outside the one resolver is the process's own reads: `seon.config/value` reads the process-wide config (`seon.config/config`, loaded once with its --set layers), so two roots in one process (the checks') cannot differ through it. Correct: a root's config in force is a layer the resolver reads (start! takes `{:set {k v}}`, resolved like a --set but per root; every reader in root's procs resolves through the root's config, not the process's), the options and with-provider go. Needs: what v1 did (a config per system), and the resolver's context naming the root. Root's gate (AGENTS §3) before building: which existing mechanism carries a per-root layer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
